-"""A copy paints exactly the colours its older table paints — lane CUTOVER-READINESS.
-
-THE USE CASE. Ridgeline HVAC's dispatcher keeps "Service Calls" in admin's Workspace and colours
-cells by hand: a call's Job goes red when it is urgent, amber when parts are on order. The
-organization has not pressed its Data tables switch, so the older table is where the table lives
-and the dispatcher keeps recolouring there. The copy on the new table page is what the switch will
-show, so every rerun of the mover must leave it painting what the older screen paints:
-
-- a cell recoloured on the older table after the first copy is recoloured on the copy;
-- a cell cleared on the older table is cleared on the copy;
-- a cell coloured for the first time is coloured on the copy;
-- a stored colour the older screen never painted ("purple" — the older grid paints only the store's
-  seven) is NAMED in the move's report, never counted as "already there".
-
-RED on the pre-lane mover (``attributes.py`` at HEAD before this lane): the attribute pass was
-additive only, so the recoloured cell kept its first colour and the cleared cell stayed coloured —
-the per-cell colours the switch showed were not the older table's. AGAINST THE DEV CLONE
-(``--records-target clone``), in one transaction that is rolled back.
-
-A person's test recolour on the copy (lane COPY-WRITABLE) stays on screen until the switch; the
-older table's colours go into the kept image the switch puts back — asserted in the second test.
-"""
