-"""A VALUE TOO BIG FOR ONE CELL MOVES AS A FILE, AND THE CELL POINTS AT IT (lane MOVER-BIG-VALUES).
-
-THE USE CASE. Harbor & Pine Mechanical, a heating-and-cooling contractor, keeps a "Heat Pump
-Field Research" table in the older data tables: for each topic an agent gathered the raw search
-text it read (one cell, ~250 KB), and a person wrote the findings summary beside it. On
-2026-09-24 the copy of four such tables in Arman's Org into the record store was refused whole,
-because one value per row was over the organization's ceiling for one value
-(``custom/value_max_bytes``) and the store says, in its own words, *"Put the big thing in a file
-record and point the value at it."* This is that sentence, done by the mover:
-
-* the whole text becomes a file of the same organization, owned by the row's author, at the
-  row's web lane (published only when the row is), with provenance naming the older table, row and column;
-* that file is a record of the kernel File Table, and the record carries an association to it;
-* the cell holds the first words, its envelope pointing at a provenance entry naming the file,
-  whose hash is the original text's.
-
-THE RED TWIN (:func:`test_without_the_rule_the_store_refuses_the_row`) lands the same row with
-the rule switched off and asserts the store refuses it with its own sentence — so the green is
-the rule's doing, not a ceiling that happened to be high.
-
-Everything happens inside ONE transaction that is rolled back: no dataset, row, file record or
-association survives the run. The bytes port is an in-transaction double of the host's file
-service (the files.files row is real and rolled back with the rest); the real service is proven
