-"""W7's gate — a column's FORMAT survives the move, or the move is not a move.
-
-THE DEFECT THIS GUARDS (OLD-TABLES-CUTOVER rev 2 §5, W7; the attacker's H1). A user
-column declares two different things and they are not the same thing:
-
-    ``data_type``          how the cell is STORED — eight words.
-    ``metadata.format``    what the column MEANS — thirty-four.
-
-``UserTableMover`` read only the first. It never selected ``metadata`` at all, so on the
-day the tables move **currency lands as a bare number, a percentage as a number nobody
-can read, a choice as free text, a formula as the string nobody typed, and a `relation`
-as a uuid** — across 116 live columns of the estate, with nothing raising, because the
-storage type was perfectly valid the whole time. A move that loses what every column
-MEANS is not a migration; it is a data loss with a report attached.
-
-WHAT IS ASSERTED. The mover's plan is built from the REAL estate on the declared target —
-every dataset, every column, as they actually are — and every column that declares a
-format must arrive carrying it. Not a sample of five written here: **all of them**, named
-individually when one is missed. The five the plan calls out by name (`choice`,
-`multi_choice`, `formula`, `currency`, `relation`) are asserted to be PRESENT in the
-estate as well, so a target that happens to hold none of them cannot pass this by being
-empty.
-
-THE RED TWIN is `test_the_old_behaviour_is_actually_refused`: it rebuilds the mapping the
-mover shipped with — `field_shape("udt", data_type)`, the storage type alone — and asserts
-every one of those five columns comes out WRONG, by name. A guard that has never been
-shown failing is not a guard.
-"""
-
-from __future__ import annotations
-
-import pytest
